{"title":"2025 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 22","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_22\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 22</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_22_(Chinese_exams)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 22 (Chinese exams)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(3D_geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (3D geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (calculus)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2.1_(alternative,_fixing_the_entire_order)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2.1 (alternative, fixing the entire order)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath_(Two_Solutions:_Calculus_+_Non-Calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by MadMath (Two Solutions: Calculus + Non-Calculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_22\">Problem 22</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 22\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three real numbers are chosen independently and uniformly at random between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. What is the probability that the greatest of these three numbers is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times each of the other two numbers? (In other words, if the chosen numbers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/0/770b013dc4456792be3a18205c279bdfb795c867.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\geq b \\geq c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"76\" height=\"17\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/0/de04e7b9198f50647eac5e0781274de4e1f194b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &gt; 2b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" />.)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/2/cb2e628fc896d9b2cb0d098f4ebe8b40c628cfba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}~\\frac{1}{12}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}~\\frac19\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}~\\frac18\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}~\\frac16\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"380\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Problem_22_.28Chinese_exams.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_22_(Chinese_exams)\">Problem 22 (Chinese exams)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 22 (Chinese exams)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three real numbers are chosen independently and uniformly at random between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. What is the probability that the greatest of these three numbers is greater than the sum of the other two numbers?\n</p><p>(The answer to this version of the problem is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, which is not one of the answer choices above.)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.283D_geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(3D_geometry)\">Solution 1 (3D geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (3D geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the three numbers be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />, and consider each possible triple <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/a/42ad1a328807bf2c10a2409ad9f3688cd4916f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,Y,Z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> as the (Cartesian) coordinates of a point in 3-dimensional space. Then, as each number is chosen independently and uniformly from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, the total space of possible points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/a/42ad1a328807bf2c10a2409ad9f3688cd4916f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,Y,Z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> is the unit cube <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/a/1aa14ec0b0ec1b6552c3b840e3020a7ff99e722e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq X,Y,Z \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" />. Using the idea of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometric_probability\" title=\"Geometric probability\">geometric probability</a>, it follows that the required probability equals the volume of the region consisting of all points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/a/42ad1a328807bf2c10a2409ad9f3688cd4916f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,Y,Z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> that satisfy the required condition, divided by the volume of the total space, i.e. the unit cube (which is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/d/f0d41fd68eff7ca9de5fcc0c5479159e6d3aef79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1^3 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />). Accordingly, it only remains to determine the region of possible points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/a/42ad1a328807bf2c10a2409ad9f3688cd4916f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,Y,Z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> and then compute its volume.\n</p><p>For simplicity, we may assume that e.g. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum - clearly, by symmetry, the corresponding regions where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum and where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> is the maximum have the same volume, and these regions overlap only where at least two of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fe66bac8ca833d3cfc06cb888d714143ac95106.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X,Y,Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> are both the maximum. In such cases, those two variables must be equal (e.g. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> will both be the maximum precisely if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/6/626f9fd13a60a172bf6e6598ed2b451b97f68565.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = Y \\geq Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"17\" />), and so each of these overlaps is either a region within a plane (as e.g. the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7db6c392957b3feb2330c792f0ef22b676cac4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = Y$\" width=\"54\" height=\"12\" /> defines a plane), or a straight line (in the special case where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/4/664ce50d92c7aa0fb01cda8b2add81a65869dfb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = Y = Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"95\" height=\"14\" />). It follows that the overlaps between the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> regions all have zero volume, so since each region has the same volume (as explained above), the answer will simply be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the volume of one of these regions, say the one where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum and the required condition is satisfied.\n</p><p>Within that region, we therefore have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/a/1aa14ec0b0ec1b6552c3b840e3020a7ff99e722e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq X,Y,Z \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/6/f267b5a723f42b00a2a3b64975edb3a214cd1b44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X \\geq Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/a/7aa1ef8ff149e1cf5cda6b1d43fd19757508bde7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X \\geq Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"17\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> to be the maximum, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615d964ade93f7fadd2868541a8088f717b332b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f425c4672901b02ad68fb78e6046b3f22156c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" /> for the required condition, but this combination of inequalities reduces to simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/4/fc4e69ceadd8a085658fc6e8b1c76bfe04c59228.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq 2Y,2Z &lt; X \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"161\" height=\"18\" />, since we automatically have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/b/4bb0cd9a660d304a4c07c9a622129b7eea670854.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Y \\geq Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e27efe117dc7ab66ec7f70d160bf5873efc3604.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Z \\geq Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"63\" height=\"17\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/c/aec7f1dd8e1d1da2a050798cfcc20124075250a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y,Z \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" />. This means the boundaries of the region are the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/0/7e03b099603bd28aa6e6a3feed4c5e884566ca96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/073102d3a36346516a6274185b5615a0be2ff65e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/6/096cf8cb0ad774f7cae6d6eafdedb9db737fbcf9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/c/33cc402150215e241bfeca836abbc9c8e71a006d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/a/b9ae647a7bdc7cac62bf005548031f5bc3efffb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />, so now by drawing the unit cube as well as those boundaries, we deduce that this region is a square-based pyramid: the base has vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/f/c9f24f93c3bb306fc52a01188d83aa6c9d26f118.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1,0,0\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58c43dc7ce527049caf45169201472f347641444.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1,0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"75\" height=\"45\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/e/e3e47eb6adb56e02da09581e3791ce29b9c44e50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"79\" height=\"45\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7c1d4e146be7d7ef42c6f40c38fe4f7bf495926.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"75\" height=\"45\" />, while the apex of the pyramid is at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />. The square base thus clearly has side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, and hence area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/9/a59bb7306ac08c9775e4fd87f91977f617d252c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"86\" height=\"48\" />; moreover, as the base lies in the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/a/b9ae647a7bdc7cac62bf005548031f5bc3efffb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />, the perpendicular height of this pyramid is the same as the perpendicular distance from the apex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> to that plane, which is obviously <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d04e7946ed34f4e67d153e3071d9eff89ed48e70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-0 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>It follows that the volume of this pyramid, i.e. of the region where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum and the required condition is satisfied, is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/f/1ef40a5d78520cbbe635de454664ca97fa5a5407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{12}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" /> (using the standard formula for the volume of a pyramid). Finally, as explained above, this means the required probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a644843ea16b0183c3c0bb7388a7cc4f0a213583.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac14}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"128\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(calculus)\">Solution 2 (calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (calculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider three independent uniform random variables <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b91abc49a9e72dfe06fe76f6f529fc2a2ef488fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y, Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/1/ab178d831a786b92cb4c9ddc2d33578223036f98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0, 1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" />. The goal is to find the probability that the maximum value is greater than twice each of the other two values. Equivalently, if we order them as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/0/770b013dc4456792be3a18205c279bdfb795c867.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\geq b \\geq c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"76\" height=\"17\" />, this is the probability that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/0/de04e7b9198f50647eac5e0781274de4e1f194b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &gt; 2b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> (which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/c/fdcee882bb7243b46333f835ed0bdd586b1dfb20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &gt; 2c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/1/67176ff95b512b7db774dfb6fe8355e72df5dc96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b \\geq c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" />). Therefore, if we assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum (but make no assumption about the order of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />), the required condition reduces (almost surely) to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615d964ade93f7fadd2868541a8088f717b332b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f425c4672901b02ad68fb78e6046b3f22156c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" />. Conversely, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615d964ade93f7fadd2868541a8088f717b332b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f425c4672901b02ad68fb78e6046b3f22156c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" />, then we will necessarily have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/e/fceeedb389c942f1a653cb44c25221f11dd2e519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y \\geq Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/9/0a9b9ea833f0285350f6f4b79a50bb181e817143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Z \\geq Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"17\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/c/aec7f1dd8e1d1da2a050798cfcc20124075250a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y,Z \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" />, and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> will indeed be the maximum.\n</p><p>Accordingly, the probability that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum and the required condition is satisfied is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/8/02872dc15917cba06a18ae9ee38c4e216b2e1431.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X &gt; 2Y \\cap X &gt; 2Z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"178\" height=\"20\" />, and then the answer will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times this probability, to account for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> total cases (the maximum can be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />, and clearly all of these cases are symmetric). Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fe66bac8ca833d3cfc06cb888d714143ac95106.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X,Y,Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> are chosen uniformly at random from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, the marginal distribution of each one is simply the uniform distribution on this interval. As they are also chosen independently, we deduce that their joint distribution is uniform over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/0/eb013301feb0e984ac7001d736af97a3065677f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\times [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"155\" height=\"20\" />, i.e. the unit cube, and hence the joint probability density is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/e/39e51290b39f26978e2a283c1cd295a4a11b2217.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\text{volume of the unit cube}} = \\frac{1}{1^3} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"257\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nFor the bounds of integration, we can first choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/6/dd602078998bf052ef4b971897696c004e96ce51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X \\in [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"20\" />, and then we require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/3/423d792f4f5d1649b264259b12768be64e66b091.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y,Z \\in [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615d964ade93f7fadd2868541a8088f717b332b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f425c4672901b02ad68fb78e6046b3f22156c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" />; as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/c/2dcf591196dc2eddee958876f85f599b9cf083f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />, the latter two conditions will also automatically give <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/6/c662bd11165da7d3e724b351e75ebb7a7d8a841c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y,Z &lt; \\frac{X}{2} \\leq \\frac{1}{2} &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"151\" height=\"39\" />, so it is both necessary and sufficient that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/e/85e8de7359286d5f366d065960ff14f2e5e1d0cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq Y,Z &lt; \\frac{X}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"113\" height=\"39\" />. Thus we can now compute\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/b/27b8db94a66b3fa8c53bdc192a5f10fafe34d898.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} P(X &gt; 2Y \\cap X &gt; 2Z) &amp;= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{\\frac{x}{2}} \\int_{0}^{\\frac{x}{2}} 1 \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &amp;= \\int_{0}^{1} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2 \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &amp;= \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{x^2}{4} \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\frac{1}{3}x^3\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\left(1^3-0^3\\right) \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{1}{12}, \\end{align*}\" width=\"379\" height=\"285\" /> and so, as explained above, the total probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/7/6a7cb470977a7290f6b2b4fa5c6d0d1117c95f0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac{1}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"128\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_2.1_.28alternative.2C_fixing_the_entire_order.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2.1_(alternative,_fixing_the_entire_order)\">Solution 2.1 (alternative, fixing the entire order)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2.1 (alternative, fixing the entire order)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Alternatively, instead of just fixing the maximum as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, we can fix the entire order of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> variables as e.g. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/a/9bac1337d65903513b5c46258648025fb14d95c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; Y &gt; Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"95\" height=\"15\" />. It then follows that the required condition reduces (almost surely) to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1c6a6db3dea43d09663cc9eb0c04e6e55b395b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"113\" height=\"15\" />, while conversely, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1c6a6db3dea43d09663cc9eb0c04e6e55b395b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"113\" height=\"15\" />, it will indeed necessarily follow that the order is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/a/9bac1337d65903513b5c46258648025fb14d95c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; Y &gt; Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"95\" height=\"15\" />, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/d/2bdedf02668cd919ad3db6cf20c45838cf36fa2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> again gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/e/fceeedb389c942f1a653cb44c25221f11dd2e519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y \\geq Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019cd2ed1c17b349887d33beed6e0dedd8ee4840.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Y &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"73\" height=\"15\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/f/0fffda50d6a90b7848a28040ea9d636c74727252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y &gt; Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Hence, similarly to above, the probability that the required condition is satisfied is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/e/5de22852c456def25d36292a52f644b18bd246da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X &gt; 2Y &gt; 2Z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"142\" height=\"20\" />, and then as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/a/9bac1337d65903513b5c46258648025fb14d95c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; Y &gt; Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"95\" height=\"15\" /> is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of the total <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82cd08410701d52745e16e266428819ac57ed1e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3! = 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"127\" height=\"14\" /> possible orders (with all of them clearly being symmetric), the answer will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times this probability. The joint probability density is still <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> as shown above, but now for the bounds of integration, we first choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, where we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/c/0ac20cb5d66806ff23f694e9d31a2d9e6ce2d812.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\geq X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"17\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/9/dc98254bceac4ee211854ad36bc19eee777e399f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; Y &lt; \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"87\" height=\"38\" />. We can now choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, again satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615d964ade93f7fadd2868541a8088f717b332b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> and of course <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/7/c97790030e6a6a1f24934648d36afd2b50eda1e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq X \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"17\" />, i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/6/2a63c2d1f3c5073ca982a9c44aef058b153dd4c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Y &lt; X \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"17\" /> (since we then automatically have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/0/9c0b0bfbfb0a5a1e2a3cc37946bd6c7aaffbabdf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y \\geq 2 \\cdot 0 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"155\" height=\"17\" />). Lastly, we need to choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/f/0fffda50d6a90b7848a28040ea9d636c74727252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y &gt; Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/8/928ee908f16b56b603bb43f0f67863936bde4575.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq Z \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"82\" height=\"17\" /> (as then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615d964ade93f7fadd2868541a8088f717b332b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> will automatically also give <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f425c4672901b02ad68fb78e6046b3f22156c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X &gt; 2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" />), i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/7/b576991dfdc6a825b4970e15f8158dcff10f11dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq Z &lt; Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"88\" height=\"17\" /> (since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/2/1e2e4dc2cf58f62277cfcbda06db59e0c99cd3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> will then automatically give <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/9/7498a5564a74dc2a3b92fb7d2eef7c8ef8606f81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z \\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"49\" height=\"17\" />).\n</p><p>Accordingly, we obtain\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/4/ef47447f720cf6c2570aee3f6ba3d98f1b1f4b1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} P(X &gt; 2Y &gt; 2Z) &amp;= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\int_{2y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 1 \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &amp;= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}y(1-2y) \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &amp;= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\left(y-2y^2\\right) \\, \\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &amp;= \\left[\\frac{1}{2}y^2-\\frac{2}{3}y^3\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ &amp;= \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 - \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3\\right) - (0-0) \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{1}{24}, \\end{align*}\" width=\"434\" height=\"312\" />\nand so, as explained above (and just as obtained in Solution 2), the required probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/5/5455ff3be8042af3ce9b746aa62902fc5109699b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{24} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac{1}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"128\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~funkCCP\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn.org\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn.org\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/XxVC6Hx2tIY\">https://youtu.be/XxVC6Hx2tIY</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8wNoOAqd5_o\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8wNoOAqd5_o</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath_.28Two_Solutions:_Calculus_.2B_Non-Calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath_(Two_Solutions:_Calculus_+_Non-Calculus)\">Video Solution 3 by MadMath (Two Solutions: Calculus + Non-Calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Ed